---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Method">
  <article class="m">
    <h1>Method</h1>
    <p class="m__lead">
      A component library is only reusable if it survives contact with someone else's brand.
      Everything here is built around that one constraint.
    </p>

    <h2>Colour is read, not chosen</h2>
    <p>
      Each pixel of the chosen image is converted to OKLab. Greys are discarded — they carry no
      hue worth keeping — and what remains is collected into thirty-six bins weighted by chroma,
      so a small area of saturated colour counts for more than a large dull one. The heaviest
      bin becomes the page hue. The heaviest bin at least sixty degrees away becomes the accent.
    </p>
    <p>
      Lightness is never taken from the image. It sits on a fixed ladder: 98.5 for the page,
      95.5 for fields, 84 for rules, 18 for ink. Contrast is therefore a property of the system
      rather than something measured afterwards and patched. Any photograph produces a palette
      you can read.
    </p>

    <h2>A literal colour fails the build</h2>
    <p>
      Components reference tokens and nothing else. The registry scans every stylesheet at build
      time and a hex value, an <code>rgb()</code> or an <code>hsl()</code> stops the build with
      the offending value named. Not a warning to be scrolled past — the site will not compile.
    </p>

    <h2>A missing prompt also fails the build</h2>
    <p>
      Every component carries the prompt that produced it, the attempts that failed first, and
      why each failed. A component whose metadata has no final prompt cannot be published at
      all. The code is the easy half; knowing which constraints to state up front is the part
      that transfers to whatever you build next.
    </p>

    <h2>Proportion</h2>
    <p>
      Type runs on a modular scale interpolated between two anchors: a 16px base at a 360px
      viewport on a 1.2 ratio, growing to 18px at 1440px on a 1.25 ratio. Spacing applies that
      same interpolation to multiples of the base, so vertical rhythm and type stay locked
      together at every width. Leading and tracking are functions of size — 1.55 down to 1.04,
      tracking tightening as type grows — because large text set at body leading is the fastest
      way to make a page look careless.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .m { max-width: var(--measure); }
  .m h1 { font-size: var(--f-4); }
  .m__lead { margin-top: var(--s-s); font-family: var(--serif); font-size: var(--f-1); line-height: var(--lh-lead); }
  .m h2 { margin-top: var(--s-l); margin-bottom: var(--s-3xs); }
  .m p { margin-top: var(--s-2xs); color: var(--text-dim); }
  .m code { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.9em; color: var(--text); }
</style>
